        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Non-Aligned Movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Worldwide, above all in the developing countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and the Pacific: every African country is a member, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> are its only members in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, it has met in summits from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>, with a Coordinating Bureau at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Its origins in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of 1955 and the Declaration of Brijuni of 1956; the founding conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in September 1961; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> years, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a> Conference of 1970, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> summit of 1979 and the split over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>; since the end of the Cold War in 1991, a voice for the Global South.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Its founders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>; V. K. Krishna Menon, the Indian diplomat who first used the phrase for the doctrine; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>, chair from 1979; later chairs as different as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> and Ernesto Samper.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The largest grouping of states after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, its 120 members holding 55% of the world’s population; major successes in decolonization, disarmament and opposition to racism and apartheid in its early years; since 1991 a forum for multilateralism and the developing world, often divided and rarely taking definitive action.</span></div>
        </div>
