    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Non-Aligned Movement</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bandung-conference.jpg" alt="Bandung Conference" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Bandung Conference <span class="kid-tale-cr">Foreign Ministry of the Republic of Indonesia, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>In the Cold War, two great powers formed two sides and tried to pull other countries onto them.</p><p>About 70 years ago, leaders of Asian and African countries met at Bandung and promised not to take either side.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru.jpg" alt="Jawaharlal Nehru" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Jawaharlal Nehru <span class="kid-tale-cr">Harry Pot, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Jawaharlal Nehru of India wanted newly free countries to keep their independence and not be pushed onto one side.</p><p>Its members have often disagreed, and some, such as India and Pakistan, have fought each other.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito.jpg" alt="Josip Broz Tito" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Josip Broz Tito <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 60 years ago, Tito of Yugoslavia and leaders of Egypt, India, Ghana and Indonesia founded it in Belgrade.</p><p>It looks for a middle way between the two great sides.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela.jpg" alt="Nelson Mandela" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Nelson Mandela <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kingkongphoto &amp; www.celebrity-photos.com from Laurel, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>It campaigned against colonial rule, racism and apartheid, a system of racial discrimination in South Africa.</p><p>Today its 120 member countries hold more than half of all the people in the world.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The idea grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>’s refusal to take sides in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> and from the Five Principles of Peaceful Coexistence, taken into the declaration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of Asian and African states in 1955.</li><li>Founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1961 on the initiative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>, Nehru, Nkrumah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, it sought a middle course between the Western and Eastern blocs and campaigned against colonialism, racism and apartheid.</li><li>With 120 members, nearly two-thirds of the members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, it is the largest grouping of states after the UN; since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> it has recast itself as a voice of the Global South.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The movement’s seventh summit was to meet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1982, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a>; after Iranian aircraft bombed Baghdad’s Al-Dura refinery to show that its airspace was unsafe, the summit was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> in March 1983.</p></aside>
    </section>

